  "body": {
    "id": "prop:strict-unknown-propensity-penalty",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Put \\(s_0=\\alpha+\\beta\\).  Suppose\n\\[\n \\gamma>s_0\n \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n d>\\frac{2s_0\\gamma}{\\gamma-s_0}.\n\\]\nThen the dimension condition implies \\(d>2s_0\\), and there are constants \\(c>0\\) and \\(n_0\\), depending only on \\(\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma,d,L\\), such that, for every \\(n\\ge n_0\\),\n\\[\n \\frac{\\rho_n^*}{\\rho_{n,e}^*}\n \\ge c n^{\\kappa},\n \\qquad\n \\kappa=\n \\frac{2\\gamma}{4\\gamma+d}\n -\\frac{2s_0}{2s_0+d}\n =\\frac{2\\{d(\\gamma-s_0)-2s_0\\gamma\\}}\n {(4\\gamma+d)(2s_0+d)}>0.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(\\rho_n^*/\\rho_{n,e}^*\\to\\infty\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "alpha",
      "beta",
      "gamma",
      "d",
      "L",
      "n",
      "rhoStar",
      "rhoKnown"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:sparse-nuisance-null-supported-lower",
      "lem:known-propensity-benchmark"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Because \\(\\gamma>s_0>0\\), division by \\(\\gamma-s_0\\) preserves strict inequalities and\n\\[\n \\frac{2s_0\\gamma}{\\gamma-s_0}>2s_0.\n\\]\nThus the assumed inequality for \\(d\\) gives \\(d>2s_0\\), so the hypothesis \\(d\\ge 2s_0\\) of \\texttt{thm:sparse-nuisance-null-supported-lower} holds.  That theorem therefore supplies constants \\(c_{mathrm u}>0\\) and \\(n_{mathrm u}\\) such that\n\\[\n \\rho_n^*\n \\ge c_{mathrm u}\n \\max\\left\\{n^{-2\\gamma/(4\\gamma+d)},\n n^{-2s_0/(2s_0+d)}\\right\\}\n \\ge c_{mathrm u}n^{-2s_0/(2s_0+d)}\n \\tag{1}\n\\]\nfor every \\(n\\ge n_{mathrm u}\\).  On the identical class, \\texttt{lem:known-propensity-benchmark} gives constants \\(0<c_{mathrm e}<C_{mathrm e}<\\infty\\) and \\(n_{mathrm e}\\) such that\n\\[\n 0<c_{mathrm e}n^{-2\\gamma/(4\\gamma+d)}\n \\le \\rho_{n,e}^*\n \\le C_{mathrm e}n^{-2\\gamma/(4\\gamma+d)}\n \\tag{2}\n\\]\nfor every \\(n\\ge n_{mathrm e}\\).  The left inequality in (2) makes division by \\(\\rho_{n,e}^*\\) legitimate, while its right inequality and (1) yield, for \\(n\\ge n_0:=\\max\\{n_{mathrm u},n_{mathrm e}\\}\\),\n\\[\n \\frac{\\rho_n^*}{\\rho_{n,e}^*}\n \\ge \\frac{c_{mathrm u}}{C_{mathrm e}}\n n^{\\frac{2\\gamma}{4\\gamma+d}-\\frac{2s_0}{2s_0+d}}.\n \\tag{3}\n\\]\nPutting the two fractions in (3) over their positive common denominator gives\n\\[\n \\frac{2\\gamma}{4\\gamma+d}-\\frac{2s_0}{2s_0+d}\n =\\frac{2\\gamma(2s_0+d)-2s_0(4\\gamma+d)}\n {(4\\gamma+d)(2s_0+d)}\n =\\frac{2\\{d(\\gamma-s_0)-2s_0\\gamma\\}}\n {(4\\gamma+d)(2s_0+d)}.\n \\tag{4}\n\\]\nThe numerator in (4) is strictly positive by the assumed dimension inequality, and both denominator factors are positive because \\(d,\\gamma,s_0>0\\).  Hence \\(\\kappa>0\\), and (3) tends to infinity.  Taking \\(c=c_{mathrm u}/C_{mathrm e}\\) proves the claim.",
